{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1117071-teaching-the-garou-vignettes-and-tasks","id":1117071,"title":"Teaching the Garou / vignettes and tasks","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-16T20:27:57-07:00","id":1117071,"title":"Teaching the Garou / vignettes and tasks","content":"I am looking for some good ideas and brainstorming on how to show in game about teaching Garou, especially Garou cubs, the essentials of being Garou. What kind of things would a Mentor or Den Father do.<br /> <br /> I am not asking about the specifics of what should be learned. The game mechanics (the Attributes, Abilities, Advantages) and basic Garou lore (Litany, Moots, Songs of the Garou) are obvious.<br /> <br /> What I am looking for are specific actions, events, or quests a Mentor/Den Father would have the cub perform in order to learn them. Lectures are one thing, but at some point the Garou cub has to go out and learn them himself or simply train. I am thinking of scenes like in The Karate Kid when Mr. Miyagi made Daniel paint the fence and wash the car which Daniel thinks is meaningless, rote work, but which in fact actually prepares him to learn since he is a) building his stamina, b) creating muscle memory, and c) teaching him basic movies. Later on he learns that the work he is done also provides spiritual lessons in addition to practical ones. We also see similar things in movies and TV in basic training (many of the things Captain Sobel did to train Easy Company in the Currahee episode of Band of Brothers was brilliant even though he was a terrible officer in other ways - when he knows Winter is promoted to Lieutenant and assigns him the task of mess officer for two weeks to show his leadership abilities, or when he orders the men to drain their canteens after the Currahee run to see if everyone obeyed orders to not drink from them).<br /> <br /> These kinds of tasks often seem like BS at the time and are resented during training, but usually afterwards the student or soldier is very thankful for them. For story purposes, they are also great vignettes which get the point across while not using up too much time.<br /> <br /> These are the kind of things I am looking for. They are very good things to cover in a general backstory/prelude of the character, or if a PC has taken Mentor it can help give the NPC more personality if there are actual stories the player has about them. Here are some specific things I think tasks can be divided into.<br /> <br /> So we have general physical conditioning and skill learning.<br /> <br /> How to understand, handle, and master Rage - from its first use to fuel a Garou's power to understanding the pain of Gaia.<br /> <br /> How to understand and use Gnosis - how it starts as this is how you fuel Gifts to understanding a Garou's connection to Gaia<br /> <br /> How to step sideways for the first time.<br /> <br /> How to understand the other forms (lupus/primal-urge for Homids, humanity and human based skills for lupus and metis) and control shapechange<br /> <br /> What it means to be in the company of Pure Breed and recognize it<br /> <br /> How to channel and use Ancestors<br /> <br /> Understand the basic concepts of chiminage and interaction with spirits<br /> <br /> Learn about each of the Auspice roles and how it affects a Garou's individual behavior, their role in a pack, their role in a sept<br /> <br /> <br /> So what kind of things have people included in their background, seen happen in game, or can think up right now?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-23T03:19:24-07:00","id":1119443,"title":"","content":"Learning the basics of the litany, and learning to perform and understand common howls, such as the howl of introduction, are important. Learning some tribal history would be important to most, if not all, of the tribes, and probably interesting to any Galliard.<br /> <br /> For lupus, learning how humans measure time has been an interesting challenge in a game I'm playing in now: days and seasons are intuitive, but the division of days into hours and minutes has had to be explained. The same would go for measuring distance in any way other than how many days it would take to get somewhere on foot. Human concepts of time and distance may or may not be important, depending on the setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13713-wilson","name":"Wilson","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"182"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-24T19:58:27-07:00","id":1120191,"title":"","content":"Good point on the hours and minutes of the day.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-01T16:33:41-07:00","id":1123691,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1117071-teaching-the-garou-vignettes-and-tasks#post1117071\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How to understand and use Gnosis - how it starts as this is how you fuel Gifts to understanding a Garou's connection to Gaia<br /> <br /> How to step sideways for the first time.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm mastering a Rite of Passage right now, and because of that the players started without having put a single foot in the Umbra yet, let alone having gifts. Because of the increased difficulty of stepping sideways after months spent in the material world, I assumed that for a werewolf was near-to-impossible reach the Umbra alone the first time, especially if we consider an inexperience malus (it would be, what, difficulty 12 or so?). I pushed my players in the Umbra through a moon bridge, but you can also use one of those gifts meant to push something corporeal through the Gauntlet (they were rank 3, i believe?).<br /> It was kinda funny, since as they step through the silver portal they would find themselves with no clothes, no money and headed to a far, far away place that would put them in an unfamiliar setting with no resources beside their own companions. I described the stepping into the Umbra as a physical sensation of awakening, like that tingle that your arms sometimes do if you slept on them. It was also written (somewhere) that the Umbra feels more real than the real world to the Garou, with stronger smells and deeper colours.<br /> <br /> I took the use of the Gifts as something natural. The spirit transfers it in your body, and at that point you're suddenly able to do it. Like grasping a bottle, now you can create fire with about the same ease. Once learnt, the gift become a natural part of the Garou, and since their spirit side kinda awakens the first time they step in the Umbra I don't think that learning how to use it should feel too intellectual. It should be more like an instinct: once you've been there and back, you can try to attune yourself again to that place and that sensation. And suddenly, you're there again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-01T21:19:35-07:00","id":1123763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1117071-teaching-the-garou-vignettes-and-tasks?p=1123691#post1123691\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm mastering a Rite of Passage right now, and because of that the players started without having put a single foot in the Umbra yet, let alone having gifts. Because of the increased difficulty of stepping sideways after months spent in the material world, I assumed that for a werewolf was near-to-impossible reach the Umbra alone the first time, especially if we consider an inexperience malus (it would be, what, difficulty 12 or so?). I pushed my players in the Umbra through a moon bridge, but you can also use one of those gifts meant to push something corporeal through the Gauntlet (they were rank 3, i believe?).</div> </div> </div> </div>The idea that a Garou would undergo their Rite of Passage without first entering the Umbra seems absurd to me. In any sept, this should be one of the first supernatural powers taught (and done under heavy supervision). It is just that basic.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't start the penalty for lunar cycles where the Garou has not spending time between the worlds until the Garou had their first change. That is when the countdown begins, not earlier. The caern is likely going to be the first place where cubs are allowed to cross the Gauntlet so difficulty will be low and success possible even for 1 Gnosis Homids. If there was some kind of massive penalty because cubs have never crossed the Gauntlet before (so the difficulty would be 10), then the obvious solution would be to either bring in a high level Theurge, or send the cubs to one, with the Gift Grasp from Beyond so that the penalty would be negated and proper teaching done (or perhaps open a Moon Bridge as you did).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I took the use of the Gifts as something natural. </div> </div> </div>Yes, that is the case as long as you learn them from spirits. They more or less just bless you and the knowledge flows automatically to you. If a Garou teaches you a Gift, it does take some time. But once it is learned, using it is automatic. But I think learning Gifts presupposes the Garou already has some earlier knowledge like &quot;this is how you release Rage or Gnosis&quot;. Those kinds of things are going to be taught way before cubs learn any Gifts. At the very least, you'll want the cub to know how to donate Gnosis to the caern totem and then regain it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T19:38:54-07:00","id":1124589,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1117071-teaching-the-garou-vignettes-and-tasks?p=1123763#post1123763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The idea that a Garou would undergo their Rite of Passage without first entering the Umbra seems absurd to me. In any sept, this should be one of the first supernatural powers taught (and done under heavy supervision). It is just that basic.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not so basic. The manual W20 &quot;Umbra, the velvet shadow&quot;, page 14, actually writes: &quot;A werewolf\u2019s first emergence into the Penumbra marks an important part of the Rite of Passage&quot;.<br /> It's also written somewhere that cubs usually learn their first gifts during the Rite, but since I can't remember where precisely I read it you'll have to just trust me on this one. =P<br /> <br /> There also is a short story at page 13, same manual, where a character named Joseph Star-treader steps for the first time into the Umbra; and since Garou names as &quot;star-treader&quot; are taken when the Totem accepts the pup into the Tribe after the Rite of Passage, I could assume that mr. Joseph completed its Rite without even reaching the Umbra.<br /> <br /> The flavour story from an old 2nd edition manual (Rite of Passage) speaks of pups facing the Rite without even knowing how to change forms. Yes, it's old and some of the things inside are conflicting what was written in the 20th manuals, but I'm mentioning it because I think it's important to understand how little the cubs may know and understand before facing their trials.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That is when the countdown begins, not earlier. </div> </div> </div>Even assuming so (I don't think there are official sources on this, it would make just as much sense counting from birth), if you assume that a young wolf is going to need one or two years to learn the basics of being a Garou before attempting the Rite of Passage (wolf-borns need time to learn at least one Human language and the Garou one, and homids still have their regular lives to take care of inbetween their running-like-wolves lessons) and if they don't usually reach the Umbra before the said rite then you can realize how the degradation of their spirit side is still going to be very real.<br /> <br /> Also I'm wondering: a metis has his first change when he's five if I correctly recall, yet he has been a garou his whole life. Shouldn't at least he count from birth?<br /> <br /> It's really a moot point anyway, since every rite of passage is handled differently from sept to sept basing on what the elders (and the storyteller) think it best fits the story. If you want them to travel in the Umbra and learn gifts before joining a tribe and facing their Rite of Passage, by all means do so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If there was some kind of massive penalty because cubs have never crossed the Gauntlet before (so the difficulty would be 10), then the obvious solution would be to either bring in a high level Theurge, or send the cubs to one, with the Gift Grasp from Beyond so that the penalty would be negated and proper teaching done (or perhaps open a Moon Bridge as you did). </div> </div> </div>I'd surely put that kind of penalty too. Entering the Umbra means to shift your own perception of reality, and especially homid Garous are just not in touch enough with the natural world to manage that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If a Garou teaches you a Gift, it does take some time. But once it is learned, using it is automatic. But I think learning Gifts presupposes the Garou already has some earlier knowledge like &quot;this is how you release Rage or Gnosis&quot;. Those kinds of things are going to be taught way before cubs learn any Gifts. At the very least, you'll want the cub to know how to donate Gnosis to the caern totem and then regain it. </div> </div> </div>I don't think that a cub ever had to give Gnosis to a Caern totem, not even sure if he had any reason to see one (they're usually summoned in moots, and I doubt that cubs would be attending them. They're not full members of the Sept yet, after all). But since Gifts are that instinctive, simply by learning them they may understand that a particular sensation weakens them on a spiritual level, and that it's exactly the thing that fuels whatever effect they now know. So, don't overthink this.<br /> <br /> Rage may be a little trickier, meaning that your characters don't have a graduated scale that points out how many rage points they have, or telling them they have a Rage score to begin with. But, since it's RAGE, I'd say that it can be triggered by simply humiliating the Garou until they're pissed enough to actually discharge one fist per second, or maybe even frenzy again. With enough sparring they should eventually realize how to channel their fury and those physical sensations that mark the limits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T22:13:34-07:00","id":1124621,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1117071-teaching-the-garou-vignettes-and-tasks?p=1124589#post1124589\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not so basic. The manual W20 &quot;Umbra, the velvet shadow&quot;, page 14, actually writes: &quot;A werewolf\u2019s first emergence into the Penumbra marks an important part of the Rite of Passage&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>My version actually says &quot;A werewolf\u2019s first emergence into the Penumbra marks an important step toward of the Rite of Passage.&quot; I don't know if your version is different. There is clearly a typo there as &quot;toward of&quot; is obviously a mistake. &quot;A step<i> toward</i> the Rite of Passage&quot; means one thing while &quot;a step of the Rite <i>of </i>the Passage&quot; means something else.<br /> <br /> It makes a lot more sense that this is something taught in preparation for their Rite of Passage. The entire point of such a rite of passage is that the person is now an adult, not a child. As a child you are taught how to do things. As an adult you contribute; you are ready to take on responsibilities. It's assumed you don't need to be taught the essentials anymore. A rite of passage for a Garou who does not know how the basic powers of his kind work seems idiotic. It'd be like giving someone a driver's license without them going through driver's education first. or a college diploma without making them take classes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1117071-teaching-the-garou-vignettes-and-tasks?p=1124589#post1124589\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There also is a short story at page 13, same manual, where a character named Joseph Star-treader steps for the first time into the Umbra; and since Garou names as &quot;star-treader&quot; are taken when the Totem accepts the pup into the Tribe after the Rite of Passage, I could assume that mr. Joseph completed its Rite without even reaching the Umbra.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it is simply more likely this was written without much thought being given by the author that the deed name implied the Garou was past their rite of passage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1117071-teaching-the-garou-vignettes-and-tasks?p=1124589#post1124589\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The flavour story from an old 2nd edition manual (Rite of Passage) speaks of pups facing the Rite without even knowing how to change forms. Yes, it's old and some of the things inside are conflicting what was written in the 20th manuals, but I'm mentioning it because I think it's important to understand how little the cubs may know and understand before facing their trials.</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually own this and don't remember that, but there are lots of odd things about that adventure in terms of this conversation. All the pregen characters already have all three gifts and tribes (and even carry fetishes!). Despite the cubs never even being able to complete their rite of passage during the course of the adventure, it is written in effect as if the PCs do have all their powers available to them after standard character creation (which assumes they've already passed the rite).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1117071-teaching-the-garou-vignettes-and-tasks?p=1124589#post1124589\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also I'm wondering: a metis has his first change when he's five if I correctly recall, yet he has been a garou his whole life. Shouldn't at least he count from birth?</div> </div> </div> </div>He is in Crinos form from birth. But the metis breed is no more or no less a Garou than a homid of lupus breed. Metis born cannot shapechange just like a homid or lupus. There is no reason to believe metis prior to their First Change are capable of anything else that homids or lupus can't do (no Rage, No Gnosis, nor any other power).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> If I understand your posts correctly, you believe cubs really aren't taught much, but they learn most things on the fly or instinctively either during or after their Rite of Passage. That just does not make sense to me.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-04T19:24:27-07:00","id":1125050,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1117071-teaching-the-garou-vignettes-and-tasks?p=1124621#post1124621\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My version actually says &quot;A werewolf\u2019s first emergence into the Penumbra marks an important step toward of the Rite of Passage.&quot; I don't know if your version is different. There is clearly a typo there as &quot;toward of&quot; is obviously a mistake. &quot;A step<i> toward</i> the Rite of Passage&quot; means one thing while &quot;a step of the Rite <i>of </i>the Passage&quot; means something else.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is... odd. I re-checked it right now and it spells exactly &quot;important part of&quot;. What version do you have? Hardback, pdf, special edition? You sure it's W20?<br /> It's not common to have this kind of discrepancies between versions, but I guess editing happens?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It makes a lot more sense that this is something taught in preparation for their Rite of Passage. The entire point of such a rite of passage is that the person is now an adult, not a child. As a child you are taught how to do things. As an adult you contribute; you are ready to take on responsibilities. It's assumed you don't need to be taught the essentials anymore. A rite of passage for a Garou who does not know how the basic powers of his kind work seems idiotic. It'd be like giving someone a driver's license without them going through driver's education first. or a college diploma without making them take classes. </div> </div> </div>But if you keep reading, at pp 14-15 of Velvet Shadow it really insists on the importance of the Second Breath, and it makes a lot more sense to me to include such a thing into the Rite itself, as an unmistakable and mystical step toward their true self (and adulthood). Also, it's not like the Rite of Passage is a driving licence exam... it's hard, it's harsh and some pups even die in the process. &quot;Essential&quot; or &quot;basic&quot; can mean a lot of things, and de facto entering the Umbra your first time means that you don't need to be taught how anymore, since they're learning it right at that moment.<br /> <br /> Also, I'd strongly suggest to adopt a way milder language and to disagree as a civilized person instead of addressing my interpretations as &quot;idiotic&quot;, especially when you have nothing except your opinion to support your view of the rules.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I actually own this and don't remember that, but there are lots of odd things about that adventure in terms of this conversation. All the pregen characters already have all three gifts and tribes (and even carry fetishes!). Despite the cubs never even being able to complete their rite of passage during the course of the adventure, it is written in effect as if the PCs do have all their powers available to them after standard character creation (which assumes they've already passed the rite). </div> </div> </div>Yea, and the warrior had a klaive I think? As I said it was kinda uncoherent, I just mentioned it to show how even in the official scenarios the level of preparation may vary a lot and it's mostly left to the storyteller.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> He is in Crinos form from birth. But the metis breed is no more or no less a Garou than a homid of lupus breed. Metis born cannot shapechange just like a homid or lupus. There is no reason to believe metis prior to their First Change are capable of anything else that homids or lupus can't do (no Rage, No Gnosis, nor any other power). </div> </div> </div>I disagree. If that were true, then Metis cubs would turn into vampires with no issue if embraced, and it seems an unlikely loophole (we're talking about this in another thread, that's why I'm mentioning it). Also, a Metis will always become a full-fledged werewolf, no chance of not experiencing the first change (unlike kinfolks). They're true garou, born and bred, and their condition is clearly different. Not sure if I'd give them Rage before the first change, but I would surely give them Gnosis.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If I understand your posts correctly, you believe cubs really aren't taught much, but they learn most things on the fly or instinctively either during or after their Rite of Passage. That just does not make sense to me. </div> </div> </div>No, not at all. I think they're taught history, philosophy (gaia vs wyrm), combat, shapeshifting, languages, sept social structure, role of the tribes, what's a caern, how to interact with spirits and the shape of the Umbra (everything, of course, depending on the dots allocation of the character: occult zero still means that you've been told there are spirits around and nothing more). As I wrote, I assume they stay pups for at least one-two years before facing the Rite, and that time will be spent studying.<br /> <br /> I merely don't think that stepping sideways is between those things, and that's also what is said my copy of the manual (oddly different from yours, but that's it).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}